data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1794233073700,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard CM Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module\",\"Service Module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth on July 24, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War geopolitical competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Space Race\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Soviet uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21, while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples from the mission led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals; surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Saturn V\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"United States\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957, the first artificial satellite, which fueled fears about Soviet technological and military capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard's May 1961 flight was suborbital.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"United States\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"NASA\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond the capabilities of existing rockets so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed mission to the Moon was meant to serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ends.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive or important, and none would be more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing large alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"In a real sense, going to the Moon would be an entire nation's effort, since all must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that space conquest deserves the best of all mankind and its peaceful cooperation opportunity may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered the question of why the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas: 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the US would go to the Moon this decade because the goal is hard and organizes the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy repeated the joint Moon expedition proposal in a September 20, 1963 speech to the United Nations General Assembly.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"The joint Moon mission idea was ultimately abandoned after Kennedy's death; an early key decision was lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module had a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had two stages: a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"The lunar orbit rendezvous design meant the spacecraft could be launched by a single Saturn V rocket then under development.\"}],\"entities\"